{{ 'dev-journal-cards.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
{{ 'dev-blog.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

{%- comment -%}
  Section: "Blog" (dev name `dev-blog`). The /blogs/<handle> index.

  NO FIGMA ARTBOARD EXISTS FOR THIS PAGE — same honest caveat as dev-article. It is COMPOSED from
  the system rather than measured, and the composition is deliberately boring: the page is a grid
  of the SAME cards the Our Story strip uses.

  THE CARD IS NOT REBUILT HERE. It renders through snippets/dev-journal-cards-card.liquid — the very
  file dev-journal-cards renders — with the tokens supplied by snippets/dev-journal-card-tokens.liquid
  and the styling by assets/dev-journal-cards.css. A reader clicking from the Journal strip to this
  index and back must not see the card change, and the only way to guarantee that is to make it the
  same card, not a lookalike. Reading time therefore also matches by construction: both go through
  snippets/dev-article-read-time.liquid, which prefers the `custom.reading_time` metafield.

  THE TAG FILTER is real data, not decoration. Shopify already routes /blogs/news/tagged/<tag>, and
  the articles already carry the tags the design uses as badges ("Guest Review", "Insight",
  "Expert Guide"). So the filter is a row of links, not JS: it works with scripting off, it is
  crawlable, and the browser's back button behaves.

  TWO PLACEMENTS, ONE SECTION — added at the owner's ask, so the Our Story page ends on the same
  header-and-grid as the blog index (minus the filter, which is already a switch).

  On /blogs/<handle> Shopify hands the template a `blog` object and this section IS the page: its
  title is the h1, the filter drives Shopify's own /tagged/ routes, and the articles paginate.
  Anywhere else — a page template — none of that holds: `blog` is nil, there is no route to
  paginate, and an h1 already sits above. So off the index the section reads its blog from a
  picker, shows a fixed count of the newest articles, drops pagination, and steps every heading
  down one level.

  It is the SAME FILE either way, not a lookalike. That is the same argument the card makes two
  paragraphs up, applied one level out: the only way two surfaces stay identical is for them to be
  one surface.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%} Tokens for the CARD — shared with dev-journal-cards so the two cannot drift. {%- endcomment -%}
{%- render 'dev-journal-card-tokens', scope: '.dev-blog' -%}

{%- comment -%} Tokens for the PAGE around the cards — this section's own {%- endcomment -%}
<style>
  .dev-blog {
    {%- comment -%} Title/intro scale mirrors dev-proudest-moments ("Our proudest moments" on Our Story) at the
       owner's ask — its measured tokens are 38px heading / 18px body on desktop and 28 / 16 on
       mobile, a ~2.1:1 contrast. Was 48 / 32 before; the intro sizes already matched. {%- endcomment -%}
    --font-title-size: 38px;
    --font-title-size-m: 28px;
    --font-title-lh: 1.2;
    --font-intro-size: 18px;
    --font-intro-size-m: 16px;

    --gap-head: 16px;
    --gap-section: 48px;

    {%- comment -%} Mobile CAP on the top spacer, not a scale factor, and the difference matters
       because this section now renders in two places whose --pt differ (48 on the blog index, 80 on
       Our Story). A 0.6 scale — dev-proudest-moments' figure — would give Our Story the 48 it needs
       and quietly drop the blog index to 29. A cap gives both 48: min(80, 48) and min(48, 48).

       48px is not a new number. It is what dev-values-we-cherish sits at on mobile (--pt-m: 48px),
       and dev-proudest-moments' 0.6 scale resolves to the same 48 — so the last section of Our Story
       now lands on the page's own mobile rhythm instead of holding its desktop 80. {%- endcomment -%}
    --pt-m: 48px;

    {%- comment -%} Leaf ornament. Sized off the client's reference image — there is no artboard for
       this page. Measured from that JPEG by reading the ink bounds rather than by eye:

         sprig      28 x 25 px
         title      cap height 51px  (=> its heading is ~73px, nearly twice ours)
         gap        9px between the sprig's foot and the cap line

       That gives two honest readings, and they differ by 2x because the reference's heading is
       so much bigger than ours:

         proportional to the TITLE   28/73 x 38  = 15px  <- reads as a speck, the sprig stops
                                                            being legible as leaves
         proportional to the PAGE    28px in a 1200 design, ~30px in our 1312 column

       Taking the page reading. An ornament's job is to decorate at page scale, and at 15px this
       one is a smudge. 28px keeps the reference's absolute presence. The 9px gap is 0.18 of the
       reference cap height; against our ~27px cap that is 5px, rounded to 4.
       Mobile steps with the heading, 38 -> 28. {%- endcomment -%}
    --leaf-size: 28px;
    --leaf-size-m: 21px;
    --leaf-gap: 4px;
    --gap-filter: 8px;
    --gap-grid: 32px;
    --gap-grid-m: 24px;

    --grid-min: 320px;

    --filter-px: 16px;
    --filter-py: 8px;
    --filter-size: 14px;
    --radius-filter: 60px;

    --pagination-gap: 8px;
    --pagination-size: 40px;
  }
</style>

{%- liquid
  comment
    Which blog, and which of the two placements are we in?

    The test is `blog.handle`, NOT `if blog` and NOT `blog == blank`. On a page template `blog` is
    simply undefined, so it is nil and any of the three would do — but `blogs[handle]` for a blog
    that does not exist returns an EmptyDrop, which is TRUTHY and whose `== blank` result is not
    something to bet a page on. Reading a property that only a real blog has is the one test that
    behaves the same for both.
  endcomment
  assign is_blog_index = false
  if blog.handle != blank
    assign is_blog_index = true
  endif

  assign blog_source = blog
  unless is_blog_index
    assign blog_source = blogs[section.settings.blog]
  endunless

  assign per_page = section.settings.articles_per_page
  assign current_tag = current_tags | first

  comment
    h1 + h2 cards where this section is the whole page; h2 + h3 where it is one strip under the
    page's own h1. Skipping a heading level is a real accessibility failure, not a style nit, and
    a second h1 on Our Story would be one too.
  endcomment
  assign title_level = 2
  assign card_level = 3
  if is_blog_index
    assign title_level = 1
    assign card_level = 2
  endif
-%}

<div
  class="dev-blog{% if section.settings.centre_head %} dev-blog--centred{% endif %}"
  style="
    --leaf: {{ section.settings.leaf_color }};
    --bg: {{ section.settings.background_color }};
    --color: {{ section.settings.text_color }};
    --muted: {{ section.settings.meta_color }};
    --chip: {{ section.settings.filter_color }};
    --chip-active: {{ section.settings.filter_active_color }};
    --chip-active-text: {{ section.settings.filter_active_text_color }};
    --rule: {{ section.settings.rule_color }};
    --pt: {{ section.settings.padding_top }}px;
    --pb: {{ section.settings.padding_bottom }}px;
  "
>
  <div class="dev-blog__inner">
    <header class="dev-blog__head">
      {%- if section.settings.show_leaf -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          The leaf sprig from the client's reference for item 15. Hand-drawn, NOT a Figma export:
          the blog has no artboard in the file (it is not in .claude/figma-shopify.json), and the
          theme carries no leaf asset — grep for leaf/sprig/branch returns only a stray word in a
          footer comment. So this is a stand-in built to the reference image: same laurel sprig,
          same up-right lean, colour sampled from the JPEG (#b08b67 at the core, opened out to
          #c5a17a for a flat fill). If the designer supplies the real vector, replace this path
          set wholesale — nothing else in the section depends on its geometry.

          Decorative only: aria-hidden, no title, no role. It repeats nothing and names nothing,
          so a screen reader must not stop on it.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          viewBox is cropped to the drawing's own ink box, measured by rasterising the paths and
          reading the alpha bounds: x 3.00, y 4.33, 18.12 x 14.68. A square 0 0 24 24 would pad the
          sprig with ~30% empty field on every side, so `width: 24px` would render a ~17px sprig and
          the ornament would read as an afterthought next to a 38px heading. Cropped, the box IS the
          artwork and --leaf-size means what it says. Aspect is 1.235:1, so height is `auto`.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <svg class="dev-blog__leaf" viewBox="3 4.33 18.12 14.68" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M3.4 18.6 Q10.80 11.70 16.42 9.66" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="0.8" stroke-linecap="round"/>
          <path d="M0 0 C1.79 -1.60 4.61 -1.60 6.40 0 C4.61 1.60 1.79 1.60 0 0 Z" transform="translate(6.84 16.32) rotate(-79.5)"/>
          <path d="M0 0 C1.76 -1.58 4.54 -1.58 6.30 0 C4.54 1.58 1.76 1.58 0 0 Z" transform="translate(11.66 13.13) rotate(-79.5)"/>
          <path d="M0 0 C1.60 -1.43 4.10 -1.43 5.70 0 C4.10 1.43 1.60 1.43 0 0 Z" transform="translate(16.47 9.94) rotate(-79.5)"/>
          <path d="M0 0 C1.74 -1.55 4.46 -1.55 6.20 0 C4.46 1.55 1.74 1.55 0 0 Z" transform="translate(9.25 14.72) rotate(12.5)"/>
          <path d="M0 0 C1.65 -1.48 4.25 -1.48 5.90 0 C4.25 1.48 1.65 1.48 0 0 Z" transform="translate(14.06 11.53) rotate(12.5)"/>
          <path d="M0 0 C1.46 -1.30 3.74 -1.30 5.20 0 C3.74 1.30 1.46 1.30 0 0 Z" transform="translate(16.42 9.66) rotate(-33.5)"/>
        </svg>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        The blog's own title, not a theme setting — rename the blog in Shopify and the page follows.
        A setting here would be a second place to keep in sync, and the first one to go stale.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The two branches are NOT interchangeable. The setting is inline_richtext — already-safe HTML
        from Shopify, so escaping it would print the merchant's italics as visible tags. blog.title is
        raw store data and must still be escaped. `default:` would have forced one filter on both.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- liquid
        # "Stack the title" puts every word of the title on its own line — "Larke Snooze" becomes
        # "Larke" / "Snooze". Done in CSS (width: min-content), not by rewriting the string with
        # <br>: the heading is inline_richtext and may carry markup whose attributes a naive
        # space-to-<br> replace would corrupt.
        assign title_class = 'dev-blog__title'
        if section.settings.stack_heading
          assign title_class = title_class | append: ' dev-blog__title--stacked'
        endif
      -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Neither branch may run when BOTH sources are empty. On the blog index that cannot happen —
        `blog.title` is always there — but off it, a freshly added instance has no heading typed and
        no blog picked yet, and the old unguarded `else` emitted `<h2 class="dev-blog__title"></h2>`:
        a heading with no accessible name sitting above the empty state. The theme's sibling section
        guards the same thing (dev-journal-cards.liquid), so this matches, rather than invents, the
        house rule.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if section.settings.heading != blank -%}
        <h{{ title_level }} class="{{ title_class }}">{{ section.settings.heading }}</h{{ title_level }}>
      {%- elsif blog_source.title != blank -%}
        <h{{ title_level }} class="{{ title_class }}">{{ blog_source.title | escape }}</h{{ title_level }}>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if section.settings.intro != blank -%}
        <p class="dev-blog__intro">{{ section.settings.intro | escape }}</p>
      {%- endif -%}
    </header>

    {%- comment -%}
      Tag filter. `all_tags` is every tag across the blog; `current_tags` is what the visitor is
      filtered to. Plain links to Shopify's own /tagged/ routes — no JS, works crawled and with
      scripting off. Hidden entirely when the blog has no tags, rather than rendering an empty rail.

      INDEX ONLY, regardless of the setting. Off the index there is no `current_tags` to reflect, so
      every chip would render unselected with "All" permanently lit — a control displaying a state it
      is not in. It is also the one thing the owner explicitly did not want on Our Story.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if is_blog_index and section.settings.show_tag_filter and blog_source.all_tags.size > 0 -%}
      <nav class="dev-blog__filter" aria-label="{{ section.settings.filter_label | escape }}">
        <a
          class="dev-blog__chip{% unless current_tag %} dev-blog__chip--active{% endunless %}"
          href="{{ blog_source.url }}"
          {% unless current_tag %}aria-current="page"{% endunless %}
        >
          {{- section.settings.all_label | escape -}}
        </a>

        {%- for tag in blog_source.all_tags -%}
          {%- assign is_active = false -%}
          {%- if current_tags contains tag -%}
            {%- assign is_active = true -%}
          {%- endif -%}

          <a
            class="dev-blog__chip{% if is_active %} dev-blog__chip--active{% endif %}"
            href="{{ blog_source.url }}/tagged/{{ tag | handleize }}"
            {% if is_active %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}
          >
            {{- tag | escape -}}
          </a>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </nav>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if is_blog_index -%}
      {%- paginate blog_source.articles by per_page -%}
        {%- if blog_source.articles.size > 0 -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            Inside the paginate block `blog_source.articles` is already the current page's slice, so
            `card_limit` here is a belt-and-braces cap, not the thing doing the paging.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- render 'dev-blog-cards',
            articles: blog_source.articles,
            card_limit: per_page,
            card_level: card_level,
            cta_label: section.settings.button_label,
            read_time_label: section.settings.read_time_label,
            author_prefix: section.settings.author_prefix,
            eager_first: true
          -%}

          {%- comment -%}
            Pagination only when there is a second page. Rendering a lone, dead "1" under a
            three-article blog is noise pretending to be a control.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- if paginate.pages > 1 -%}
            <nav class="dev-blog__pagination" aria-label="{{ section.settings.pagination_label | escape }}">
              {%- if paginate.previous -%}
                <a class="dev-blog__page dev-blog__page--arrow" href="{{ paginate.previous.url }}" aria-label="{{ section.settings.previous_label | escape }}">
                  <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="99 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                    <path d="M106 16L102 12L106 8M102 12L120 12" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                  </svg>
                </a>
              {%- endif -%}

              {%- comment -%}
                The comparison string is built in its own assign. A filter pipe is NOT allowed inside
                a condition — `elsif part.title == paginate.current_page | append: ''` parses as
                `elsif part.title == paginate.current_page` and silently DISCARDS the rest, so every
                non-link part would have fallen through to the "…" branch and the current page number
                would have rendered as a greyed-out gap.

                A non-link part is either the current page or the ellipsis; nothing else. Comparing
                against the current page tells the two apart.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              {%- assign current_page_title = paginate.current_page | append: '' -%}

              {%- for part in paginate.parts -%}
                {%- if part.is_link -%}
                  <a class="dev-blog__page" href="{{ part.url }}">{{ part.title }}</a>
                {%- elsif part.title == current_page_title -%}
                  <span class="dev-blog__page dev-blog__page--current" aria-current="page">{{ part.title }}</span>
                {%- else -%}
                  <span class="dev-blog__page dev-blog__page--gap">{{ part.title }}</span>
                {%- endif -%}
              {%- endfor -%}

              {%- if paginate.next -%}
                <a class="dev-blog__page dev-blog__page--arrow" href="{{ paginate.next.url }}" aria-label="{{ section.settings.next_label | escape }}">
                  <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="99 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                    <path d="M116 16L120 12L116 8M120 12L102 12" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                  </svg>
                </a>
              {%- endif -%}
            </nav>
          {%- endif -%}

        {%- else -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            Reachable in two ways: a blog with no posts, or a /tagged/ route with no matches. The
            second needs a way back, or the visitor is stranded on a dead end with the filter still
            showing their selection.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div class="dev-blog__empty">
            <p>{{ section.settings.empty_text | escape }}</p>
            {%- if current_tag -%}
              <a class="dev-blog__chip" href="{{ blog_source.url }}">{{ section.settings.all_label | escape }}</a>
            {%- endif -%}
          </div>
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endpaginate -%}

    {%- else -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Off the blog index: a fixed count of the newest articles, no paginate block and no
        pagination nav. There is no route to page through here — /pages/our-story?page=2 is not a
        thing anyone should be able to reach by clicking — so the control is not rendered rather
        than rendered dead.

        `| plus: 0` on the count is not decoration. With no blog picked — the state every freshly
        added instance of this section starts in — or one that was later deleted, `blogs[handle]` is
        an EmptyDrop, `.articles.size` on it is nil, and `nil > 0` is a comparison Liquid refuses
        rather than a falsy answer. Coercing to a number first makes that case render the empty
        state instead of an error string in the theme editor.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- assign source_count = blog_source.articles.size | plus: 0 -%}
      {%- if source_count > 0 -%}
        {%- render 'dev-blog-cards',
          articles: blog_source.articles,
          card_limit: section.settings.articles_count,
          card_level: card_level,
          cta_label: section.settings.button_label,
          read_time_label: section.settings.read_time_label,
          author_prefix: section.settings.author_prefix,
          eager_first: false
        -%}
      {%- else -%}
        <div class="dev-blog__empty">
          <p>{{ section.settings.empty_text | escape }}</p>
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Blog",
  "tag": "section",
  "settings": [
    { "type": "header", "content": "Content" },
    { "type": "inline_richtext", "id": "heading", "label": "Heading", "info": "Leave empty to use the blog's own title." },
    { "type": "checkbox", "id": "stack_heading", "label": "Stack the heading", "default": false, "info": "Puts each word of the heading on its own line — \"Larke Snooze\" becomes \"Larke\" over \"Snooze\". Off, the heading renders on one line as before." },
    { "type": "checkbox", "id": "centre_head", "label": "Centre the header", "default": false, "info": "Centres the heading, the intro line and the tag filter. This is the layout in the client's reference image for the blog header." },
    { "type": "checkbox", "id": "show_leaf", "label": "Show the leaf ornament", "default": false, "info": "Adds the laurel sprig above the heading, as in the client's reference. Drawn to match that image — the blog has no Figma artboard and the theme carries no leaf asset, so replace it if the designer supplies the real vector." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "intro", "label": "Intro line", "default": "Sleep, trees and everything in between." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "empty_text", "label": "Empty state message", "default": "Nothing here yet — check back soon." },

    { "type": "header", "content": "Cards" },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The cards are the same ones the Journal section uses. Reading time comes from each article's 'Reading time' metafield, and falls back to an estimate from the word count."
    },
    {
      "type": "blog",
      "id": "blog",
      "label": "Blog",
      "info": "Only used when this section sits on a page rather than on the blog itself — on the blog it always shows that blog. Pick one here for the Our Story page."
    },
    { "type": "range", "id": "articles_per_page", "min": 3, "max": 24, "step": 3, "label": "Articles per page", "default": 9, "info": "Blog page only." },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "articles_count",
      "min": 1,
      "max": 12,
      "step": 1,
      "label": "Articles to show",
      "info": "Used when this section sits on a page. Three fills one row; more will wrap. There is no pagination outside the blog.",
      "default": 3
    },
    { "type": "text", "id": "button_label", "label": "Card link label", "default": "Read Article" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "read_time_label", "label": "Reading time label", "default": "min read" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "author_prefix", "label": "Author prefix", "default": "By" },

    { "type": "header", "content": "Tag filter" },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_tag_filter",
      "label": "Show the tag filter",
      "info": "Blog page only — the chips filter by Shopify's tag routes, which a page has none of. Built from the blog's real tags, and hidden automatically when there are none.",
      "default": true
    },
    { "type": "text", "id": "all_label", "label": "\"All\" chip label", "default": "All" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "filter_label", "label": "Filter label (screen readers only)", "default": "Filter articles by topic" },

    { "type": "header", "content": "Pagination" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "pagination_label", "label": "Pagination label (screen readers only)", "default": "Pagination" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "previous_label", "label": "Previous page label (screen readers only)", "default": "Previous page" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "next_label", "label": "Next page label (screen readers only)", "default": "Next page" },

    { "type": "header", "content": "Colors" },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "background_color",
      "label": "Background",
      "info": "Transparent by default so the page canvas shows through, as on the Journal section.",
      "default": "rgba(0,0,0,0)"
    },
    { "type": "color", "id": "text_color", "label": "Text color", "default": "#333333" },
    { "type": "color", "id": "meta_color", "label": "Intro and meta text", "default": "#666666" },
    { "type": "color", "id": "filter_color", "label": "Filter chip background", "default": "#FCF5E3" },
    { "type": "color", "id": "filter_active_color", "label": "Filter chip background (selected)", "default": "#333333" },
    { "type": "color", "id": "filter_active_text_color", "label": "Filter chip text (selected)", "default": "#FFFFFF" },
    { "type": "color", "id": "rule_color", "label": "Rules and borders", "default": "#C4BCA9" },
    { "type": "color", "id": "leaf_color", "label": "Leaf ornament", "default": "#C5A17A", "info": "Sampled from the client's reference image. The theme's own sand tone is #C4BCA9 if you would rather it matched the rules and borders." },

    { "type": "header", "content": "Spacing" },
    { "type": "range", "id": "padding_top", "min": 0, "max": 120, "step": 4, "unit": "px", "label": "Padding top", "default": 48 },
    { "type": "range", "id": "padding_bottom", "min": 0, "max": 120, "step": 4, "unit": "px", "label": "Padding bottom", "default": 80 }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Blog articles",
      "settings": {
        "centre_head": true,
        "show_leaf": true,
        "show_tag_filter": false,
        "articles_count": 3,
        "padding_top": 80,
        "padding_bottom": 0
      }
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
